|~ `::frame`, the kind of one level of the trace a failure leaves, and
   what is declared under it: the refusal of a payload that holds no
   subject and steps, and the verbs that read a frame.
   The rules it holds: [QSERR#trace]. ~|

:frame
  |~~ A frame is one level of the trace a failure leaves, the step that
      failed there with the value it met: the subject of the step, the
      steps of its level from the one that failed, and, where the step ran
      its code on a part of its subject, the path of that part.
      `::frame["x" ~(add 1 | mul 2)]` is `add 1`, which failed on `"x"`,
      and `mul 2`, which did not run; `::frame[[1 "x" 3] ~(map ~(mul 2)) /1]`
      is `map`, which failed where its code ran on the element at `/1`.

      A trace holds a frame for each step that raised a failure or handed
      it on, a frame a line, and a frame prints on one line whatever its
      subject holds. `subject`, `unfinished`, `failed`, `skipped` and
      `place` read a frame, and a path reads it as the vector it is, `/0`
      its subject, so `!| get /trace/1/0/1` reads the element the code of
      `map` failed on. The steps skipped at the level that reads a failure
      run again on another value by `apply`.
      ::snippet~([1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trace | last)
      ::link~(::trace | doc) ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | wraps |~~ The subject, the steps and the place. ~~| ::vec
    | holds :PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError
        |~~ A frame holds the subject a step met and the quote of the
            steps from the one that failed, one step at least, and after
            them the path of the part of the subject the step ran its code
            on, where it ran it on one: `::frame["x" ~(add 1)]`,
            `::frame[[1 "x"] ~(map ~(mul 2)) /1]`.
        ~~|
        ~(allOf ~(count | gte 2) ~(count | lte 3) ~(get /1 | is ::quote) ~(get /1 | count | gt 0) ~(anyOf ~(count | eq 2) ~(get /2 | is ::path)))
    | proves :holdsTheStepThatFailedAndTheValueItMet
        |~~ The frame of `add 1` holds the value it met. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trace | first | eq ::frame["x" ~(add 1)])
    | proves :holdsThePlaceOfThePart
        |~~ The frame of `map` holds the place of the element its code
            failed on.
        ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trace | last | eq ::frame[[1 "x" 3] ~(map ~(mul 2)) /1])
    | proves :printsOnOneLine
        |~~ A frame prints on one line whatever its subject holds. ~~|
        ~({:a {:b [1 2]}} | get /c !| trace | first | print | lines | count | eq 1)
    | proves :PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError/refusesASubjectAlone
        |~~ A subject with no steps is no frame. ~~|
        ~(::frame["x"] !| type | eq ::frame/PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError)
    | proves :PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError/refusesAPlaceThatIsNoPath
        |~~ A place that is no path is refused. ~~|
        ~(::frame["x" ~(add 1) 1] !| type | eq ::frame/PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError)
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAMap
        |~~ A map is no frame. ~~|
        ~(::frame{:failed ~(add 1)} !| type | eq ::frame/PayloadKindNotTakenError)

:frame/subject
  |~~ Answers the subject of the frame, the value the step that failed
      there met, `::frame["x" ~(add 1)] | subject` answering `"x"`: the
      first frame of a trace holds the subject the step that failed met, and the
      frame of a verb that ran its code on a part of its subject the whole
      subject, its `place` naming the part.
      ::link~(::frame/place | doc) ::link~(::frame/failed | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The value the step met, of any kind. ~~| ::any
    | throws :SubjectNotFrameError
        |~~ `subject` reads a frame, which the trace of a failure holds,
            `"x" | add 1 !| trace | first | subject`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheValueTheStepMet
        |~~ The first frame holds the subject the step that failed met. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trace | first | subject | eq "x")
    | proves :answersTheWholeSubjectOfAVerb
        |~~ The frame of `map` holds the vector it ran its code over. ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trace | last | subject | eq [1 "x" 3])
    | proves :SubjectNotFrameError/refusesAnError
        |~~ An error at rest is read by `trace` first. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| subject !| type | eq ::frame/subject/SubjectNotFrameError)
    | impl ~(get /0)

:frame/unfinished
  |~~ Answers the steps of the level of the frame from the one that
      failed, a quote: the step that failed and the steps the failure
      skipped after it, `::frame["x" ~(add 1 | mul 2)] | unfinished`
      answering `~(add 1 | mul 2)`. `failed` answers its first step and
      `skipped` the rest.
      ::link~(::frame/failed | doc) ::link~(::frame/skipped | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The steps from the one that failed, a quote of one step at
            least.
        ~~|
        ::quote
    | throws :SubjectNotFrameError
        |~~ `unfinished` reads a frame, which the trace of a failure holds,
            `"x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trace | first | unfinished`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheStepsFromTheOneThatFailed
        |~~ The steps left unfinished are the step that failed and the
            steps skipped after it.
        ~~|
        ~(::frame["x" ~(add 1 | mul 2)] | unfinished | eq ~(add 1 | mul 2))
    | proves :holdsTheStepThatFailedAndTheStepsSkipped
        |~~ `failed` and `skipped` read the two parts of it. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trace | first | get [~(unfinished) ~(failed) ~(skipped)] | eq [~(add 1 | mul 2) ~(add 1) ~(mul 2)])
    | proves :SubjectNotFrameError/refusesAMap
        |~~ A map is no frame. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | unfinished !| type | eq ::frame/unfinished/SubjectNotFrameError)
    | impl ~(get /1)

:frame/failed
  |~~ Answers the step that failed, the quote of its one step,
      `::frame["x" ~(add 1 | mul 2)] | failed` answering `~(add 1)`, so
      `!| trace | map ~failed` reads the trace of a failure a step at a
      time, from the step that refused to the step of the level that
      reads it. The step is code that runs again by `apply` and is written
      as text by `toString`, and `failed | first` reads the record of its form,
      a `::call`.
      ::link~(::frame/skipped | doc) ::link~(::frame/unfinished | doc)
      ::link~(::trace | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The step that failed, a quote of its one step. ~~| ::quote
    | throws :SubjectNotFrameError
        |~~ `failed` reads a frame, which the trace of a failure holds,
            `"x" | add 1 !| trace | first | failed`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheStepThatFailed
        |~~ The step that failed is the first of the steps of the frame. ~~|
        ~(::frame["x" ~(add 1 | mul 2)] | failed | eq ~(add 1))
    | proves :readsATraceAStepAtATime
        |~~ The steps of a trace read from where the failure arose outward.
        ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trace | map ~failed | eq [~(mul 2) ~(map ~(mul 2))])
    | proves :readsTheRecordOfItsStepByFirst
        |~~ The first of the quote is the record of the step, a call. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trace | first | failed | first | type | eq ::call)
    | proves :SubjectNotFrameError/refusesAQuote
        |~~ A quote is no frame. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | failed !| type | eq ::frame/failed/SubjectNotFrameError)
    | impl ~(unfinished | take 1)

:frame/skipped
  |~~ Answers the steps the failure skipped after the step that failed, a
      quote, the empty quote where it skipped none:
      `"x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trace | last | skipped` answers `~(mul 2)`,
      the steps left at the level that reads the failure, which run again
      on another value by `apply`.
      ::link~(::frame/failed | doc) ::link~(::frame/unfinished | doc)
      ::link~(::any/apply | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The steps skipped, a quote. ~~| ::quote
    | throws :SubjectNotFrameError
        |~~ `skipped` reads a frame, which the trace of a failure holds,
            `"x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trace | last | skipped`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheStepsSkipped
        |~~ The steps after the one that failed did not run. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trace | last | skipped | eq ~(mul 2))
    | proves :answersTheEmptyQuoteForNone
        |~~ A step that skipped none answers the empty quote. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trace | last | skipped | eq ~())
    | proves :runsAgainByApply
        |~~ The steps skipped run again on another value. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trace | last | skipped | :rest it | 5 | apply rest | eq 10)
    | proves :SubjectNotFrameError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector is no frame. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | skipped !| type | eq ::frame/skipped/SubjectNotFrameError)
    | impl ~(unfinished | drop 1)

:frame/place
  |~~ Answers the path of the part of the subject the step ran its code on
      where it failed, null where it ran it on no part:
      `[1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trace | last | place` answers `/1`. A
      verb that runs its code on the elements of its subject, `map`,
      `filter`, `every`, `any` and `reduce`, and the keys of `sort`,
      `min`, `max`, `groupBy` and `indexBy`, names the place of the
      part, the path of its index or of its key, and `update` the path it
      wrote; the
      subject reads the part by it, `get (f | place)` with `f` the frame.
      ::link~(::frame/subject | doc) ::link~(::path | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The path of the part, null where the step ran its code on none. ~~| #[::null ::path]
    | throws :SubjectNotFrameError
        |~~ `place` reads a frame, which the trace of a failure holds,
            `[1 "x"] | map ~(mul 2) !| trace | last | place`.
        ~~|
    | proves :namesTheElementTheCodeFailedOn
        |~~ `map` names the index of the element its code failed on. ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trace | last | place | eq /1)
    | proves :namesTheKeyOfAMap
        |~~ Over a map it names the key. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b "x"} | map ~(add 1) !| trace | last | place | eq /b)
    | proves :namesThePathUpdateWrote
        |~~ `update` names the path it wrote. ~~|
        ~({:a {:n "x"}} | update /a/n ~(add 1) !| trace | last | place | eq /a/n)
    | proves :answersNullWhereNoCodeRanOnAPart
        |~~ A step that ran no code on a part names none. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trace | first | place | eq null)
    | proves :readsThePartFromTheSubject
        |~~ The subject of the frame reads the part by its place. ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trace | last | :f it | subject | get (f | place) | eq "x")
    | proves :SubjectNotFrameError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no frame. ~~|
        ~(5 | place !| type | eq ::frame/place/SubjectNotFrameError)
    | impl ~(query /2)
